    "done_when": "tests/test_stall_window_respects_a_child.py holds a live pointer whose worker process has a sleeping child, advances the clock past fifteen minutes, then asserts no stalled transition; it then kills the child, asserting the stalled transition on the next snapshot. A second case holds a live worker with no child, asserting the stalled transition past fifteen minutes as today. Both the ticker path and the watch generator are exercised. Every process the test starts is recorded by pid and terminated in a finally block, with the test asserting none survives it. The first case fails at the base revision with a stalled transition at fifteen minutes (logged) and passes at the head. The negative control drops the descendant term in a scratch copy; the first case fails, with the mutation on the red log's first line and an EXIT= line after pytest's output. The gate is the new test plus the existing test files that exercise the stall window; it exits 0, or shows no added failing ids against the base, with both arms recorded as log paths under baseline_suite and after_suite, each ending in an EXIT= line. The test neither reads nor writes the real ~/.config/reckon.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Plan a-stalled-row-says-whether-the-worker-lives §6: a live worker with a running job is not stalled at fifteen minutes. Read §6 in docs/plans/a-stalled-row-says-whether-the-worker-lives.html. Extend the stall window to the run's budget_seconds where the worker process is alive with a live descendant process; a run whose worker has no live descendant keeps the fifteen-minute window. The stall decision today compares quiet against stall_seconds with no descendant term, on the ticker path near reckon/crew/recovery.py:5270 with its counterpart in the watch generator near :6162 (line numbers predate today's recovery.py merges, so find both sites by that comparison). Put the descendant read beside the module's other worker-liveness reads, so one seam answers whether the worker lives as well as whether something under it does.",
